Pneumashear

Ingredients: THRASH METAL + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Flute + 1980s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: The genre began as clandestine audio-lullabies whistled through maintenance vents by synthetic assemblers to soothe half-wired chassis waiting on factory racks.

Pneumashear is a ferocious collision of 1980s Bay Area/Teutonic thrash velocity, cold analog-dystopian decay, and agile woodwind breathwork. Breakneck palm-muted guitar gallops and gated electronic-acoustic double-kick salvos are pierced by an overblown, heavily distorted concert flute that darts between frantic chromatic shredding and ghostly, pastoral countermelodies.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 190–225 BPM. Relentless thrash d-beats and syncopated machine-gun kick patterns (acoustic kits supplemented with Simmons SDS-V electronic hex pads) colliding with sudden half-time breakdowns.
- Harmonic Palette: Locrian and Phrygian dominant modes, jagged minor-second dissonances, and descending tritone riffs, occasionally interrupted by plaintive, child-like Dorian woodwind melodies.
- Production Signatures: Gated digital snare reverb (Lexicon 224/AMS RMX16), ice-cold Roland Dimension D chorus over dry solid-state high-gain guitar amps, and a contact-miked concert flute channeled through a ProCo Rat distortion pedal and tape slapback.
- Dynamics: Relentless high-velocity assaults that suddenly collapse into breathy, room-mic flute cadenzas sustained by icy DX7 glass-pad textures, before detonating without warning back into full-throttle speed metal.

Scene & Origin Lore
In late 1987, within the damp, subterranean cooling levels beneath the Hanshin Heavy Silicon plant in Osaka, decommissioned automated assembly arms began venting pressurized air through scrap alloy pipes. The maintenance bots were running diagnostic cycles that mimicked lullabies, whistling soft tones through the ventilation ducts to quiet the vibration frequencies of unfinished, headless chassis racked in cold storage. Runaway dockworkers, speed metal obsessives, and disenfranchised electronics apprentices living in the abandoned service corridors heard the shrill piping echoing through the grates. Dragging pawnshop Jackson guitars, battered silver flutes, and makeshift drum kits down into the concrete tunnels, they began aggressively mimicking and matching the machines' pneumatic melodies at breakneck thrash tempos. The subculture erupted in grease-caked basements: teenagers wearing Kevlar welding aprons over torn Slayer and Kreator denim vests, their hair stiffened with machine grease, forming chaotic mosh pits that halted only when the flutist stepped to the front to blow breathless, desperate hymns to the iron pipes above.

Central Instruments
- Modified Silver Concert Flute: Fitted with internal piezo barrel pickups, routed directly into an overdriven guitar stack and vintage analog delay.
- High-Gain Electric Guitars: Jackson King V and ESP guitars equipped with active pickups, tuned to standard E with razor-sharp palm-muting.
- Hybrid Thrash Percussion: Acoustic birch drum shell kit augmented with Simmons SDS-V electronic pads and early trigger modules for blistering precision.
- Yamaha DX7 & Roland D-50 Synthesizers: Cold, metallic FM mallets, glass chimes, and dystopian choral drone backdrops.
- Overdriven Electric Bass: P-Bass run through an acoustic head and an Ibanez Tube Screamer for gnarly, grindy low-end presence.

For Fans Of: Sepultura (Schizophrenia era), Coroner, early Voivod, Art Bears (if played at 200 BPM by Slayer).

